course: dictionary
lesson: 1977
title: Giving and taking compliments
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A compliment is something nice you say to praise someone or show you admire them. «Your drawing of the fox is brilliant» is a compliment. To compliment someone is to say something nice about them or about what they did.
explain: When someone compliments you, say «Thank you» instead of arguing. «Thanks, I worked hard on it» is kind and fine too. Saying «No, it's rubbish» makes the other person feel silly for being nice to you.
explain: The best compliments are specific and true. «You kept trying even when the sums were hard» says more than just «You're great». Compliment things people choose or do, like their effort, kindness or drawing, rather than how they look or their weight.
explain: In stories, a compliment can show kindness, or it can be a trick. Sometimes a character praises someone only to get something they want. If someone keeps hoping to be praised, that is called fishing for compliments. Ask yourself whether the praise is honest.
fact: A compliment is a small gift of words.
fact: «Compliment» can be a noun (a nice thing you say) or a verb (to say it): «She complimented me on my kindness.»
fact: «Compliments» in a formal letter means good wishes, as in «with our compliments».
fact: Useful phrases: pay someone a compliment, take it as a compliment, compliment someone on something.
fact: A good compliment is specific and true, so it tells the person exactly what they did well.
exercise: «Your drawing of the fox is brilliant.» What is this? | A warning | An order | *A compliment | A question
exercise: When someone compliments you, what is a polite thing to say? | Prove me wrong | No, it's rubbish | Nothing, and look away | *Thank you
exercise: Which is the better compliment? | You have the best face in the class | You are nicer than everyone | *You kept trying even when the sums were hard
test: «Ben complimented Sara on her kind help.» What does complimented mean here? | *Said something nice about Sara | Ignored Sara | Told Sara off | Gave Sara a book
test: Which phrase means to say something nice about someone? | Lose your way | *Compliment someone on something | Miss the bus | Fish for compliments
exam: Priya says, «Thanks, I worked really hard on it.» Why is this a good reply to a compliment? | It changes the subject to lunch | It argues with the person | *It is polite and accepts the praise | It ignores the compliment
